# Formology — OWL 2 DL Ontology (v0.4.0 draft)

*Reflects the ending core after both research passes. Key change from v0.3.0:
Proformation and Conformation are no longer disjoint classes — they are
replaced by a single relation resolvedBy(form, context). Level is derived
as chain length. See docs/ending-core.md for the full revised hypotheses.*

@prefix : <http://formology.org/ontology#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://formology.org/ontology> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Formology" ;
    rdfs:comment "The study of form as the atomic unit. Form is what stays the same across a declared range of variation, in a declared respect. A form is fixed but underspecified; its licensed readings are settled by the contexts in force for it." ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.4.0-draft" .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 1. ROOT CLASS — Form (H1, revised)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:Form a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Form" ;
    rdfs:comment "What stays the same across a declared range of variation, in a declared respect. The range is set by the comparison level, not by the form. 'Form' names what a resolution selects; it is distinct from what gets resolved (the proformation)." .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 2. TWO KINDS OF FORM (H4, revised — replaces the three-condition test)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:Placeholder a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Placeholder" ;
    rdfs:comment "A form that stands in for a recoverable antecedent (anaphoric (b)): pronouns, blank fields, 'do so.' The narrow linguistic proformation." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Form .

:Pattern a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Pattern" ;
    rdfs:comment "A form with at least one parameter left open: types, open terms, notations, 'issue.' The broad case." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Form .

# Excluded from Form: anchors and limits (H8)
:Anchor a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Anchor" ;
    rdfs:comment "Not a form. Content from itself: proper names, one-time keys, brute facts, genotypes as causes. What contexts bottom out in." ;
    owl:disjointWith :Form .

:Limit a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Limit" ;
    rdfs:comment "Not a form. Nothing to read: the unmarked state. What the apparatus is defined against." ;
    owl:disjointWith :Form .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 3. RESOLUTION AS A RELATION (H5, revised — replaces disjoint classes)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# NOTE: In v0.3.0, Proformation and Conformation were disjoint classes.
# The collaborator review showed this contradicts recursion (H5) and
# fails formology's own cases. They are now roles in a resolution relation.

:resolvedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "resolved by" ;
    rdfs:comment "A resolution relation: resolvedBy(form, context). 'Proformation' = unresolved relative to a context; 'conformation' = resolved under it. Disjointness holds per record, not per thing." ;
    rdfs:domain :Form ;
    rdfs:range :Form .  # context is also a Form (H3 revised — context names a role, not a kind)

# Level is derived as chain length, not primitive:
# level(resolvedBy(f, c)) = 1 + level(c)
# This cannot be expressed in OWL without reification; see practical-implications.md

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 4. ROLES IN A RESOLUTION (H3, revised — dropped as ontological claim)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# H3 as an ontological claim ("context is not form") is DROPPED.
# Every context formology names is itself a form by H4's own test.
# "Context" and "form" name roles in a resolution, not kinds of thing.
# Both roles are filled by forms. Context for a form at level N is
# form at level N+1 held fixed for the purpose of resolving N.

:hasRole a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has role in" ;
    rdfs:comment "A form plays the 'form' role or the 'context' role in a given resolution. Both roles are filled by forms." ;
    rdfs:domain :Form .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 5. RECURSION (H5, revised)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:containsForm a owl:ObjectProperty, owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:label "contains form" ;
    rdfs:comment "A resolved form is a form at the next level, possibly with emergent structure no input had. There is no self-resolving top (Tarski, Girard). The lowest levels are degenerate, not absent." ;
    rdfs:domain :Form ;
    rdfs:range :Form .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 6. SUPERSSESSION (H6, dropped — replaced by "supersedable with trace")
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# H6 as stated ("resolution can be reversed") is DROPPED.
# Resolution can be superseded, not undone. Withdrawal leaves a trace,
# and the trace is a form. There is no return to the prior proformation.

:supersededBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "superseded by" ;
    rdfs:comment "A resolution record may be superseded by a new resolution whose context refers to the old one. The old record is not deleted; the trace persists as a form." ;
    rdfs:domain :Form ;
    rdfs:range :Form .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 7. POSIT (H7, revised)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# "There is form" is a chosen posit (Fichte's Setzen, Spencer-Brown's
# injunction), not a consequence of the nature of form. Every claim
# built on it is disconfirmable in practice: by register rejection,
# nesting failure, or invariance failure. Consistency confirms only
# non-contradiction. The intent/outcome gap belongs to systems under
# study, never to formology's own predictions.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 8. REGISTER (H9, new)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

:Register a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Register" ;
    rdfs:comment "A second resolution record on an already-resolved thing, indexed to the reader's role. Whether one form underlies several registers is decided per case by whether the shared sense does work." ;
    rdfs:subClassOf :Form .

:hasReader a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has reader" ;
    rdfs:comment "The reader's role determines the register. Register lives in the reader, not in the word." ;
    rdfs:domain :Register ;
    rdfs:range :Form .
